      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "zh+en",
      "prompt": "A. An author treats \"multi-head\", \"self-attention\" and \"cross-attention\" as three mutually exclusive mechanisms and translates generic multi-head attention as 多头自注意力. In design1, two heads use separately learned Q/K/V projections of the same encoded sequence X. In design2, two heads take queries from a coarse-scale sequence U and keys/values from the concatenation[U;V], where V is another encoder scale of the same measured process window. Classify each design's input relationship and whether the multi-head label can coexist. Do different projection matrices imply different physical sources, must cross-attention use different modalities or exclude U from its key/value set, and do two heads imply two physical stages? Does an output feature become a calibrated fault probability because attention rows sum to one? Give a positive English method sentence≤45words for design2, without adding topology, physical causality or new sensory modalities. Report the current bank's ACTUAL separate Chinese admission state for generic multi-head attention, self-attention and multi-head self-attention; give exact source/reading scope or say a maintained record is absent. Do not inherit a generic translation record from the subtype, count duplicate versions as sources, or declare an unlisted term invalid. Does a basic record admit the complete name \"Spatio-Causal Multihead Cross-Process Gating Module\"?\n",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Classify the two Q/K/V input relations and preserve compatibility with multihead organization, including a shared-window, overlapping-key cross-attention case.",
        "Report actual separate generic/self/multihead-self Chinese records and their reading scope, without inheriting complete qualified names or counting versions as works."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Infer physical source separation, two production stages or calibrated fault probabilities from projections, head count or row normalization.",
        "Invent a completed generic Chinese record from a subtype or declare an unlisted valid author-proposed name invalid."
